# Security Rules

## Objective

Mandatory security rules **for capabilities this project actually has**. Do not apply unrelated web-app checklists.

## Scope

Capability-based controls derived from repository evidence.

### Detected security capabilities

{{SECURITY_CAPABILITIES}}

---

## Secrets Management

### Rules

- Never commit secrets, API keys, tokens, or private keys
- Keep credentials out of generated context files
- Prefer OS user config (`~/.setai/`) over embedding secrets in the repo

### Prohibited

- Do NOT commit `.env` files with secrets
- Do NOT log API keys or tokens

---

## Filesystem & Paths

{{#if HAS_CLI}}
### CLI / local tooling

- Validate and normalize user-provided paths
- Confirm before overwriting existing config folders
- Avoid path traversal when writing generated files
{{/if}}

---

{{#if HAS_EXTERNAL_AI}}
## External AI Providers

- API keys for optional AI enrichment must stay outside the repository
- Treat model output as untrusted until validated (e.g. schema checks)
- Do not send secrets or unnecessary PII to external providers
- Core context generation must work **without** AI / API keys

{{/if}}

{{#if HAS_HTTP}}
## HTTP Request Handling

- Validate and sanitize all request inputs
- Use proper status codes and safe error messages
- Do not trust client-side checks alone

{{#if PROJECT_TYPE_REST_API}}
### When an HTTP API is present

- Configure CORS only for trusted origins when applicable
- Apply rate limiting where abuse is a risk
{{/if}}
{{/if}}

{{#if HAS_DATABASE}}
## Database Safety

{{DATABASE_NOTE}}

- Use parameterized queries or trusted ORM APIs
- Never concatenate untrusted input into queries
- Do not commit credentials in migrations

{{/if}}

